# Cloudflare Pages response headers — STATIC ASSETS ONLY.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE ADDING A RULE FOR `/` OR `/api/*`: Cloudflare does not apply
# _headers to responses generated by Pages Functions, "even if the request URL
# matches a rule defined in _headers"
# (developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/headers/). On this project `/`
# and every /api/* path ARE Functions, so a rule here for those paths would look
# correct in review, deploy without error, and do nothing at runtime. Their
# headers come from lib/http-headers.js, applied in the Function code.
#
# What genuinely reaches the static asset server, and is therefore covered here:
#   /install.sh, /install.ps1   — via the 200-rewrites in _redirects (a direct
#                                 request for the path, not the `/` negotiation)
#   /landing.html               — if requested by name rather than through `/`
#   /favicon.svg, /feed/*       — plain files
#
# Kept deliberately in sync with BASE_SECURITY_HEADERS in lib/http-headers.js.
# If you change one, change the other: they are two enforcement points for one
# policy, and the whole reason both exist is that neither covers everything.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), camera=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains

# The landing page is the only document this project serves. Same policy as
# DOCUMENT_CSP in lib/http-headers.js: one inline <script>, two inline <style>,
# no external origins, one same-origin fetch, one same-origin form post.
#
# BOTH spellings are required and `/landing` is the one that actually matters.
# The asset server canonicalises `/landing.html` to `/landing` with a 308, so
# the URL a browser ends up on — and the only one that renders a document — is
# `/landing`. Measured on install.kaeva.app 2026-08-20: `/` with Accept:
# text/html returns `308 location: /landing`, and `/landing` returns 200
# text/html. A rule on `/landing.html` alone would only ever decorate the
# redirect, never the page.
/landing
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none'

/landing.html
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none'

# The installers are piped straight into a shell. A CSP is meaningless to curl,
# but nosniff and a correct type matter to anyone who opens the URL in a browser
# first to read it — which is the behaviour we want to encourage.
/install.sh
  Content-Type: text/x-shellscript; charset=utf-8
  Content-Disposition: inline

/install.ps1
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
  Content-Disposition: inline

# The signed update feed is consumed by the desktop updater, cross-origin.
/feed/*
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=60

# The *.pages.dev deployment aliases must never be indexed — install.kaeva.app
# is the only canonical door. Each Pages deployment also gets a per-commit
# <hash>.unblock-install.pages.dev alias, hence both patterns.
https://:project.pages.dev/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex

https://:version.:project.pages.dev/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
